Why an in-process LLM was load-bearing in the first place — and the
specific things that made removing it land cleanly:

The embedded path existed because llama.cpp was the easiest way to ship
"zero-config local inference" before Ollama was as polished as it is
now. It has paid for itself in pain: invariant-violation crashes inside
`llama-cpp-2` (KV-cache type mismatches and sampler lifetime issues
were the recurring two) brought down the whole server process, and
releasing the desktop bundle required cross-compiling with the right
combination of `metal`/`cuda`/CPU per platform. CUDA in particular
needed bespoke `CUDA_PATH`/`RUSTFLAGS` setup in build.sh to find
libcudart_static.a across distros — the find_cuda_helper crate only
searches `<root>/lib64` paths, which misses Debian/Ubuntu multiarch
layouts. All of that is gone now.

What hooked the deletion into the rest of the system cleanly:

- `provider: ollama` already existed and worked end-to-end through
  LocalProvider (the HTTP-proxy provider, not the embedded one — the
  naming was confusing because "local.rs" hosts the Ollama HTTP client).
  Dropping `provider: local` was a single match arm, not a rewrite.
- The reconciler's `reconcile_models` already pulls per-base_url for
  every Ollama-using agent (added in PR #102's pass). That's now the
  *only* model-acquisition path. No new endpoint needed.
- `cached_gguf_path` and `model_path` were threaded through complete_setup
  and add_agent, both of which I'd touched in PR #102. The blast radius
  was contained — three call sites updated.

Things I deliberately did NOT do:

- Did not introduce `POST /api/setup/pull-model` with SSE progress.
  Tempting, but the reconciler already pulls and the wizard's "agent
  will start replying once Ollama finishes pulling" message is honest
  about the asynchrony. Deferred to a follow-up if anyone complains.
- Did not collapse `system::detect` / `system::recommend_model` to drop
  GPU detection. The recommendations still feed the Ollama tag picker,
  and an Apple-Silicon-with-32GB-of-unified-memory user genuinely benefits
  from being told "you can run a 27B model" rather than "qwen3.5:4b".
  The function used to exist for picking GGUF quantization tiers; now
  it picks Ollama tag size. Same numbers, different downstream consumer.
- Did not rename `local.rs` → `ollama.rs`. The struct is `LocalProvider`
  and used in places I'd need to chase. Renaming buys consistency but
  costs review noise; bundled into a future janitorial pass.
- Did not delete the `local_model` / `local_base_url` field names from
  the wizard's request body. They're still used — they're now Ollama-
  only — and renaming them would force the frontend to update.
  Documented as Ollama-only in the struct comments.

ADR shape: ADR-011 is the ADR that introduced this stuff and I marked
it "Superseded by ADR-023" with an explanatory note (matching ADR-002's
existing pattern of an inline note above ## Context). ADR-023 has the
full rationale.

The XpressAI HuggingFace org has GGUF builds (see
https://huggingface.co/XpressAI/Qwen3.6-27B-RYS-UD-Q4_K_XL-GGUF) that
outperform base Qwen3.5/3.6 — using them now requires republishing to
Ollama Hub. The user flagged this as separate work; ADR-023's "Future"
section captures it.

